WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.240
Hi, in this video I assume you know how post-graph based SLAM works and we now want to look into

00:07.240 --> 00:12.240
the question how we can use that in order to do graph based SLAM with landmarks.

00:12.240 --> 00:18.080
That means that our graph doesn't only contain pose nodes but they can also represent landmarks,

00:18.080 --> 00:22.280
so distinct points in the environment and how we can build a map of the environment

00:22.280 --> 00:27.200
that consists of those distinct points and localize our platform within such a map.

00:27.200 --> 00:31.720
So just a very brief recap on what post-graph SLAM was.

00:31.720 --> 00:35.400
So we had that vehicle, that mobile robot, that autonomous car that is driving around

00:35.400 --> 00:39.900
through the environment and after a while we always create so-called pose nodes here

00:39.900 --> 00:43.880
shown by those blue triangles.

00:43.880 --> 00:48.720
They describe the position of the platform at a certain point in time and those constraints

00:48.720 --> 00:53.960
are connected via uncertain constraints, so-called soft constraints.

00:53.960 --> 00:58.240
So we can generate those consecutive constraints but we can also generate constraints when

00:58.240 --> 01:03.900
the platform revisits a previously known place and we can generate those loop-closing constraints

01:03.900 --> 01:06.580
as we see them over here.

01:06.580 --> 01:10.760
And so the key idea of the post-graph was to have a graph where every node in the graph

01:10.760 --> 01:15.680
consists of a pose that the robot has been at a certain point in time during the mapping

01:15.680 --> 01:20.840
process and every edge in this graph represents the spatial constraints between those nodes.

01:20.840 --> 01:26.860
So it's typically a relative transformation, how one node can be transformed into a second

01:26.860 --> 01:30.760
node or relative constraints between them.

01:30.760 --> 01:37.280
And the overall idea of this post-graph-based SLAM is to find a configuration of those nodes

01:37.280 --> 01:43.280
in the graph that minimizes the error introduced by the constraints because those spatial constraints

01:43.280 --> 01:45.340
may act against each other.

01:45.340 --> 01:50.360
This results from sensor noise and also from maybe imperfect data associations.

01:50.360 --> 01:53.360
And so this was done with the so-called post-graph.

01:53.360 --> 01:58.120
So in this post-graph the vertices or nodes were always robot poses.

01:58.120 --> 02:04.380
So x, y and orientation theta for the 2D case or x, y, z and three rotational angles, your

02:04.380 --> 02:07.940
pitch roll for example, in the three-dimensional case.

02:07.940 --> 02:12.840
And in the post-graph we built the edges between those nodes through these virtual observations

02:12.840 --> 02:18.000
so by observing the environment from two different places or two different points in time and

02:18.000 --> 02:22.320
if we know that it's the same environment we can say something about the relative transformation

02:22.320 --> 02:27.000
between the two recording positions even if they happen at different points in time.

02:27.000 --> 02:33.080
That was the post-graph and we now know how we optimize such a post-graph so that we get

02:33.080 --> 02:37.480
a minimum error configuration using the tools of least squares.

02:37.480 --> 02:42.740
What we want to look into today is how can we do this if we don't have only post-nodes,

02:42.740 --> 02:47.160
we also want to represent landmarks, it means distinct points in the environment.

02:47.280 --> 02:53.600
We are not condensing this kind of local map information into this post-graph-like structure.

02:53.600 --> 02:58.240
So consider a landmark as some distinct point in the environment that we can recognize.

02:58.240 --> 03:02.340
So you have here this example of the mobile moving around and you have some landmarks

03:02.340 --> 03:08.080
or feature observations, you can see this as lighthouses as your landmarks and you're

03:08.080 --> 03:12.000
on a boat driving in a bay and you can see the different lighthouses and you want to

03:12.000 --> 03:16.560
build a map so localize where those lighthouses are and estimate your own pose.

03:16.560 --> 03:21.000
This is a traditional example for a landmark-based SLAM problem.

03:21.000 --> 03:25.280
You may not be on a boat, you can also do that by a vehicle driving through the environment.

03:25.280 --> 03:30.640
So what you see here is an aerial image of a very famous dataset, Victoria Park dataset

03:30.640 --> 03:38.080
recorded by the people at ACFR in Sydney several years back and in this dataset you see a lot

03:38.080 --> 03:44.480
of trees in this park and there was a laser scanner mounted on a vehicle tracking or estimating

03:44.480 --> 03:50.400
the trunks of the trees which served as landmarks or features generated from the scan and every

03:50.400 --> 03:54.880
feature corresponding to a landmark tree and then the task was to build a map of that environment.

03:54.880 --> 03:59.880
This was one of the datasets which has traditionally been used in the context of an EKF-based SLAM

03:59.880 --> 04:05.360
but of course can also be used here for a graph-based SLAM with landmarks.

04:05.360 --> 04:10.720
So what we can do now is we can add additional nodes to our graph and so that every of those

04:10.720 --> 04:15.600
landmarks, every of those tree trunks, becomes an own position in the map.

04:15.600 --> 04:23.120
So if we do this in the idea of the graph-based SLAM, not only having only poses, which is

04:23.120 --> 04:29.040
typically a position and an orientation, but we now have additional feature or landmark

04:29.040 --> 04:30.520
positions that we have.

04:30.520 --> 04:34.560
So this is given here by these black stars.

04:34.560 --> 04:41.600
So the difference now is that while the robot poses typically is a position and heading

04:41.600 --> 04:47.480
information, so x, y is a position and theta, the orientation of the platform, as a heading

04:47.480 --> 04:52.640
or in 3D corresponding x, y, that yawpitch role, the position of the features in the

04:52.640 --> 04:56.720
environment are typically just positions, so don't contain any orientation information.

04:56.720 --> 05:00.360
So it's just x, y or x, y is that.

05:00.360 --> 05:08.720
And so that's different because then we don't have the full 3D transformations with position

05:08.720 --> 05:11.340
and orientation information between those edges.

05:11.340 --> 05:16.560
So the edges between the robot poses and the edges between the robot pose and the landmark

05:16.560 --> 05:18.280
are different.

05:18.280 --> 05:22.680
And so we have different types of edges and different types of nodes now in our graph.

05:22.680 --> 05:26.800
So you can see this is a more complex graph where you have different types of nodes and

05:26.800 --> 05:29.120
different types of edges.

05:29.120 --> 05:35.560
So in this landmark graph for the SLAM problem, nodes can now represent two things, robot

05:35.560 --> 05:40.360
poses at different points in time and landmark locations, so positions.

05:40.360 --> 05:45.360
And the edges between those nodes can be landmark observations, that means they connect a robot

05:45.360 --> 05:53.060
pose with a landmark, meaning the robot has seen a landmark from that pose, or they can

05:53.060 --> 05:59.080
be odometry measurements, that means the traditional kind of consecutive links that we had already

05:59.080 --> 06:03.560
in our pose graph, so pose-to-pose constraint.

06:03.560 --> 06:07.760
So these odometry measurements are pose-to-pose constraints, could of course also be generated

06:07.760 --> 06:13.880
from scan matching of consecutive scans if your sensor allows for this.

06:13.880 --> 06:19.360
But what we're interested here are those landmark locations, so the observations of the robot

06:19.360 --> 06:24.040
at a certain point, seeing a landmark somewhere in the environment.

06:24.040 --> 06:28.560
And again, we can treat the minimization in graph-based SLAM very similar as we did

06:28.560 --> 06:29.560
it before.

06:29.560 --> 06:33.560
We want to find a new configuration of the nodes in the graph, and now the nodes are

06:33.560 --> 06:41.200
robot poses and landmark locations, so that the overall error is minimized that results

06:41.200 --> 06:42.200
from the constraints.

06:42.200 --> 06:46.960
So the kind of error function that is associated to all of those constraints is again summed

06:46.960 --> 06:52.280
up, squared error is computed, and then we want to find the configuration of the nodes

06:52.280 --> 06:57.680
that minimizes the errors introduced by the edges or by the constraints.

06:57.680 --> 07:02.280
So it's a very similar overall idea, the main difference in here is that the graph structure

07:02.280 --> 07:03.280
now looks different.

07:03.280 --> 07:07.480
So we don't have the pose graph, it's only poses and pose-to-pose constraints, we have

07:07.480 --> 07:13.540
robot poses and landmarks as nodes, and robot landmark observations or pose-to-pose constraints

07:13.540 --> 07:16.760
for example from odometry information.

07:16.760 --> 07:23.080
So as an example, if you have 2D landmarks, the landmark can be 2D xy point in the environment,

07:23.080 --> 07:28.400
two coordinates, and the relative observation can be the difference in the xy plane.

07:28.400 --> 07:34.180
Could be through a range in bearing observation or could be a sensor where this is converted

07:34.180 --> 07:39.560
already in a relative xy position, so delta x delta y from the current platform given

07:39.560 --> 07:40.920
its heading.

07:40.920 --> 07:45.920
And so the key thing is now how do such additional edges look like?

07:45.920 --> 07:48.640
This is something we haven't discussed so far.

07:48.640 --> 07:53.640
For the pose-to-pose constraints, we have the edges that we used in the post-graph-based

07:53.640 --> 07:58.960
SLAM, but now we have the additional edges, so in the next minutes I will just focus on

07:58.960 --> 08:06.520
how do those robot position or robot pose landmark constraints actually look like, assuming

08:06.520 --> 08:11.960
here we have a so-called xy sensor which can measure the xy location of the platform.

08:11.960 --> 08:18.360
So similar to the pose-graph-based SLAM, we have our expected observation, and this expected

08:18.360 --> 08:23.400
observation obviously depends on where is the robot in the environment, so this is now

08:23.400 --> 08:29.580
an xy theta vector, and the landmark location which is now just an xy vector.

08:29.580 --> 08:36.280
So be aware that these are different, of different dimensions, so the one is two-dimensional,

08:36.280 --> 08:40.860
the other one is three-dimensional, or in the 3D world it would be six degrees of freedom

08:40.860 --> 08:43.640
worth of three degrees of freedom.

08:43.640 --> 08:46.400
And so how is one translated into the other?

08:46.400 --> 08:52.700
So we can say this expected observation is basically given by taking the landmark location,

08:52.700 --> 08:58.900
subtracting the translation vector of the robot location, so the xy coordinate of this

08:58.900 --> 09:06.540
part, and then multiplying it with the transpose of the rotation matrix encoding the orientation

09:06.540 --> 09:08.120
of the robot's pose.

09:08.120 --> 09:14.880
And this is a way to generate this virtual observation.

09:14.880 --> 09:22.280
So this virtual observation here would then be a 2D vector, which is the outcome from

09:22.280 --> 09:27.800
that, which is the expected xy observation that the platform should see.

09:27.800 --> 09:30.520
So relative xy location.

09:30.520 --> 09:36.080
And this we can then use to directly derive the error function, so we can say my expected

09:36.080 --> 09:40.800
observation minus the actual observation that I obtained assuming I have this xy sensor,

09:40.800 --> 09:48.480
and then we just take this equation over here, put it, replace it with z hat ij, and put

09:48.480 --> 09:53.180
in this equation over here and subtract from this my actual observation.

09:53.180 --> 09:54.180
And that's it.

09:54.180 --> 09:59.140
So for the simple case of having an xy sensor which tells me where the landmark is in a

09:59.140 --> 10:05.880
relative xy coordinate frame given the robot's pose, this is how I can compute this observation.

10:05.880 --> 10:07.800
And obviously it depends on the sensor.

10:07.800 --> 10:08.800
Which sensor do I have?

10:08.800 --> 10:12.660
Do I have this type of xy sensor, yes or no?

10:12.660 --> 10:15.900
Another typical sensor that you may have, which provides actually less information than

10:15.900 --> 10:21.080
the xy sensor, is a bearing sensor, which just tells you in which direction you can

10:21.080 --> 10:22.620
actually see a landmark.

10:22.620 --> 10:27.720
So that's actually similar to a camera just in 2D where you can basically see you just

10:27.720 --> 10:33.520
have an image which just has one row, and basically the pixel lights up where you see

10:33.520 --> 10:35.740
a landmark in the kind of simplest case.

10:35.740 --> 10:42.580
So what you get is just a bearing, so just an angular observation that you have.

10:42.580 --> 10:47.140
So the landmark is still a 2D point in the environment because it still is a point somewhere

10:47.140 --> 10:51.140
in space, but now my observation is just a 2D observation.

10:51.140 --> 10:55.760
Basically what is the relative heading of the landmark with respect to the robot?

10:55.760 --> 10:59.480
So if I'm the robot, I'm looking forward, then a landmark being directly in front of

10:59.480 --> 11:01.900
me would give me an observation of zero.

11:01.900 --> 11:08.700
If I would for example be 10 degrees to the left, then I would get 10 degrees as my observation.

11:08.700 --> 11:12.160
We compute our predicted observation in order to estimate what the robot should see.

11:12.160 --> 11:16.280
We need to know where the robot is and where it's looking to, which is again stored here

11:16.280 --> 11:20.980
in xi, and we need to know where this 2D landmark is in the environment.

11:20.980 --> 11:26.320
So the landmark location is again a 2D vector shown over here, exactly the same as previously

11:26.320 --> 11:28.100
in the xy sensor.

11:28.100 --> 11:33.260
The only difference is that this function that had is now a one-dimensional function,

11:33.260 --> 11:34.700
not a two-dimensional function.

11:34.700 --> 11:42.660
So it has a 1D output just providing the angular component and not xy or delta xy as it was before.

11:42.660 --> 11:46.740
So how do we compute where the robot is and where it sees the landmark?

11:46.740 --> 11:54.500
So we can compute this by looking to the difference in the y-coordinate and the difference in the

11:54.500 --> 12:01.460
x-coordinate between the robot's location and the landmark, which is here given by xj

12:01.460 --> 12:02.460
minus ti.

12:02.460 --> 12:06.980
So this is again the transformation vector or translation vector resulting from the robot's

12:06.980 --> 12:11.860
pulse, and we are just taking the y-coordinate into account and the x-coordinate and computing

12:11.860 --> 12:13.140
the etan of that.

12:13.140 --> 12:17.620
So if you implement this, you would use of course the etan2 function in order to do this,

12:17.620 --> 12:23.980
in order to have all the quadrants done right, which tells you what is the angle between

12:23.980 --> 12:29.140
the robot's pulse with respect to the landmark in the global reference frame, and what we

12:29.140 --> 12:33.900
need to subtract from this is the orientation of the robot because there's obviously an

12:33.900 --> 12:37.940
observation which is done relative to the robot's current heading.

12:37.940 --> 12:41.540
So this is basically in the global coordinate frame and this turns it into the local coordinate

12:41.540 --> 12:45.500
frame because we need to see it as the robot would see it.

12:45.500 --> 12:49.380
So this is my expected observation function or my observation function which computes

12:49.380 --> 12:54.580
me the expected observation and from this I can then directly derive the error function

12:54.580 --> 12:59.160
because the error function was just the predicted measurement minus the actual measurement.

12:59.160 --> 13:01.220
So this is then straightforward.

13:01.220 --> 13:07.140
My error function for that bearing-only observation is what I'm predicting to see, which is this

13:07.140 --> 13:10.140
part over here, minus what I'm actually seeing.

13:10.140 --> 13:14.260
So what was the observation of this landmark j.

13:14.260 --> 13:18.340
And again this is a 1D function in this case.

13:18.340 --> 13:24.380
The XY sensor was a 2D function compared to the post-graph observation in 2D which

13:24.380 --> 13:28.840
was the 3D function that we get out of here.

13:28.840 --> 13:35.420
And this actually impacts the H matrix that we generate for this observation.

13:35.420 --> 13:39.900
Again this was computed by having the Jacobian transposed times the information matrix times

13:39.900 --> 13:40.940
the Jacobian.

13:40.940 --> 13:43.700
This gives me the element for this H matrix.

13:43.700 --> 13:47.380
So we need to look a little bit into the rank of this H matrix because this turns out to

13:47.380 --> 13:50.000
become important or is an important information.

13:50.000 --> 13:57.900
So the question is what is the rank of this Hij matrix for the 2D landmark post-constraint.

13:57.900 --> 14:03.080
If we have a matrix let's say A transposed A and when we compute the rank of this matrix

14:03.080 --> 14:10.940
A transposed A then this rank is equal to the rank of A transposed and the rank of A.

14:10.940 --> 14:17.540
And this is important because this matrix H is built up by taking the Jacobian transposed

14:17.540 --> 14:22.660
times the Jacobian of course with the information matrix in between but assuming the information

14:22.660 --> 14:29.740
matrix itself has full rank then the rank of this H matrix is basically determined by

14:29.740 --> 14:32.580
the rank of your Jacobian.

14:32.580 --> 14:40.540
And if you look to the blocks of this Jacobian this is at most a two by five matrix which

14:40.540 --> 14:42.620
is non-zero.

14:42.620 --> 14:48.660
So five for the parameters x, y, theta of the robot and x, y of the landmark and 2D

14:48.660 --> 14:51.100
because it provides you 2D information.

14:51.100 --> 14:54.540
So Hij can't have a rank more than two.

14:54.540 --> 14:58.500
So at maximum we can kind of fix two degrees of freedom in here.

14:58.500 --> 15:06.620
And if you look to the bearing only constraint and compute the H matrix then even more extreme

15:06.620 --> 15:10.560
you have a one by five matrix.

15:10.560 --> 15:17.180
So because you have one degree of freedom for the measurement and five non-zero elements

15:17.180 --> 15:21.960
for x, y, theta of the robot and x, y of the platform.

15:21.960 --> 15:25.300
So Hij in this case only a rank of one.

15:25.300 --> 15:31.300
And this has an impact on what information such a measurement can provide.

15:31.300 --> 15:35.580
And I want to show this with a small example saying where is the robot assuming we know

15:35.580 --> 15:38.460
where the landmark is and what now localize the robot.

15:38.460 --> 15:43.540
And let's see for the first example that it is an x, y observation.

15:43.540 --> 15:47.460
So the robot observed an x, y landmark and we have this relative x, y sensor.

15:47.460 --> 15:52.700
So if you have one single observation where can the robot be relative to that landmark?

15:52.700 --> 15:56.960
So assuming this black dot here is a landmark and assume we have a delta x, delta y sensor.

15:56.960 --> 16:00.980
So where can the robot actually be in the x, y plane?

16:00.980 --> 16:04.580
And it turns out that the robot can actually be at multiple places and would get the same

16:04.580 --> 16:05.780
observation.

16:05.780 --> 16:10.700
So the robot can basically be arranged in a circle around that landmark and the position

16:10.700 --> 16:14.620
of the circle then determines where the robot is actually looking to and will look like

16:14.620 --> 16:15.620
this.

16:15.620 --> 16:19.540
So the robot can be anywhere around that circle and if I fix the position on the circle it

16:19.540 --> 16:21.300
will tell me where the robot should look to.

16:21.300 --> 16:28.100
So for example if the landmark is directly in front of me then all these positions here,

16:28.100 --> 16:32.060
the right possible positions will point directly towards this landmark.

16:32.060 --> 16:36.140
So the landmark is right of me, this robot would look in this direction, this robot would

16:36.140 --> 16:37.540
look in this direction.

16:37.540 --> 16:47.340
So this rank 2 constraint allows me to fix two of the three parameters that determines

16:47.340 --> 16:49.060
my robot location.

16:49.060 --> 16:54.180
So I can be anywhere on that circle, it's a 1D solution space and the orientation is

16:54.180 --> 16:57.100
determined by where I am on that circle.

16:57.100 --> 17:00.620
And now we can look how does it look like if we have a bearing only observation.

17:00.620 --> 17:05.380
So exactly the same setup but now we only have one direction we look into.

17:05.380 --> 17:11.380
So this is our landmark location and I just know where the robots see the landmark relative

17:11.380 --> 17:13.940
to each other in terms of one rotational angle.

17:13.940 --> 17:19.540
In this case the robot can actually be anywhere in the 2D space and depending on the position

17:19.540 --> 17:23.260
of the 2D space we are actually fixing where the robot is looking to.

17:23.260 --> 17:26.740
So again if you think the landmarks directly in front of the robot but you have no idea

17:26.740 --> 17:31.500
how far it is away, the robot can be anywhere in the space and wherever it is it will point

17:31.500 --> 17:32.820
directly to the landmarks.

17:32.820 --> 17:35.540
So if it's sitting here the robot will be looking here, if the robot is here it will

17:35.540 --> 17:37.020
be looking over here.

17:37.020 --> 17:41.140
So just as a few examples these are possible locations and all the robots would look towards

17:41.140 --> 17:42.420
this landmark.

17:42.420 --> 17:47.420
So any space in the X, any position in the XY plane is possible and if you select the

17:47.420 --> 17:53.480
position then we actually fix the orientation of that platform.

17:53.480 --> 18:00.760
And so in this example the rank of this constraint is one, the one dimension of the Jacobian

18:00.760 --> 18:03.560
is just one dimensional, so rank one.

18:03.560 --> 18:07.760
We have three degrees of freedom that we need to fix for the robot pose so we need to have

18:07.760 --> 18:13.240
at least observations to kind of three landmarks in order to fix the robot pose.

18:13.240 --> 18:16.900
This is kind of the minimum that we actually need.

18:16.900 --> 18:20.640
So I hope this example, although this was basically fixed a landmark and estimating

18:20.640 --> 18:25.680
where the robot poses, has told you something that we need further information than one

18:25.680 --> 18:35.160
single observation in order to be able to deal with the optimization problem in the

18:35.160 --> 18:37.760
context of graph-based SLAM with landmarks.

18:37.760 --> 18:40.880
And the rank of the observation here plays an important role.

18:40.880 --> 18:46.240
So the rank of H of any matrix is always less or equal than the sum of the ranks of the

18:46.240 --> 18:47.240
individual constraints.

18:47.840 --> 18:53.040
And if you remember, H was built up by summing up the ranks of the individual constraints.

18:53.040 --> 18:59.880
So you will basically, the rank of H will be smaller or equal than the rank of the individual

18:59.880 --> 19:00.880
observations.

19:00.880 --> 19:04.400
So that means typically we need to have a large number of observations to actually make

19:04.400 --> 19:07.880
sure that H has its full rank.

19:07.880 --> 19:09.360
Why are we interested in full rank?

19:09.360 --> 19:13.500
Because only then we actually obtain a unique solution.

19:13.500 --> 19:17.820
So we want to have a full rank, otherwise we may not be able to fix all the parameters.

19:17.820 --> 19:20.260
You can see this, for example, within bearing-only observation.

19:20.260 --> 19:25.840
If you've seen one landmark only once from one location, you only know where this landmark

19:25.840 --> 19:27.100
lies on the line.

19:27.100 --> 19:30.740
And any position on this line is a valid solution to your problem.

19:30.740 --> 19:32.100
So this is not unique.

19:32.100 --> 19:37.720
So you can't determine this landmark location fully, only constrain it to a line.

19:37.720 --> 19:45.120
And this leads to the problem that we have of having these constraints that doesn't allow

19:45.120 --> 19:53.400
us to fully fix the system that we have in order to fully determine the solution.

19:53.400 --> 19:57.360
So a few questions.

19:57.360 --> 20:03.300
So if the rank of H is smaller or equal than the sum of the ranks of the individual constraints

20:03.300 --> 20:09.300
then we need to have a full rank in order to complete the unique solution.

20:09.300 --> 20:16.940
So how many 2D landmark observations do we need to resolve the robot's pose?

20:16.940 --> 20:23.900
And again, in order to fix a robot's pose, like what we had before, we need two observations,

20:23.900 --> 20:25.620
minimum of two landmarks.

20:25.620 --> 20:31.180
And if we are in the bearing-only case, we need to have three as a minimum, of course

20:31.180 --> 20:33.160
assuming we're living in a 2D plane.

20:33.160 --> 20:40.360
If we are in the 3D space, then we need to adapt that because we have six degrees of

20:40.360 --> 20:41.360
freedom to fix.

20:41.360 --> 20:46.160
But then depending on observations, we may have two orientation angles, for example,

20:46.160 --> 20:49.980
for the bearing-only case that we are fixing.

20:49.980 --> 20:54.240
Whatever we do in practice, we often end up with an underdetermined.

20:54.240 --> 21:01.240
So there's no guarantee for a full rank system, especially in this landmark-based situation

21:01.240 --> 21:02.240
that we have here.

21:02.320 --> 21:04.840
And landmarks may be observed, for example, only once.

21:04.840 --> 21:09.020
You might even have effects that the robot might have no odometry between two observations.

21:09.020 --> 21:15.000
If you have that case, you will suffer from this problem also in the pose graph.

21:15.000 --> 21:19.280
But if you always have odometry, then that should be okay in the pose graph point of

21:19.280 --> 21:20.280
view.

21:20.280 --> 21:25.960
But there's still something that you can do in order to deal with such systems.

21:25.960 --> 21:34.520
And this is by adding an additional damping factor to H that is multiplied with an identity

21:34.520 --> 21:35.520
matrix.

21:35.520 --> 21:40.160
So if you think about your original system was H times delta X equals minus B, that was

21:40.160 --> 21:42.940
a linear system that you wanted to solve.

21:42.940 --> 21:49.520
What you can do now is you can add an identity matrix to this matrix H plus a small scaling

21:49.520 --> 21:51.280
factor.

21:51.280 --> 21:57.840
So in this setup, you're very likely to have a full rank matrix because the identity matrix

21:57.840 --> 21:59.240
has full rank.

21:59.240 --> 22:05.920
And so by adding, let's say, a small scalar times this identity matrix, this is likely

22:05.920 --> 22:08.680
to have full rank.

22:08.680 --> 22:14.960
And if you make lambda small, you still have a full rank matrix and may not change the

22:14.960 --> 22:15.960
system too much.

22:15.960 --> 22:24.120
So again, if the lambda here gets very small, it doesn't mean that you have a well-conditioned

22:24.120 --> 22:26.660
system but at least it has full rank.

22:26.660 --> 22:33.040
And then you can actually solve this system and you may vary this parameter depending

22:33.040 --> 22:35.640
on the quality of your solution.

22:35.640 --> 22:37.960
So the question is what is the effect of doing this?

22:37.960 --> 22:43.520
The interesting thing is that what you do here is basically a combination of two approaches.

22:43.520 --> 22:49.960
So H delta X minus B is what we used from Gauss-Newton.

22:49.960 --> 22:58.100
But if you think about delta X equals minus B and you expand minus B, knowing that minus

22:58.100 --> 23:04.720
B is minus Jacobian transposed information matrix, the error, and minus the error is

23:04.720 --> 23:09.880
the residual, what this basically does, you multiply the residual with the information

23:09.880 --> 23:10.880
matrix.

23:10.880 --> 23:15.520
You multiply it based on the importance, and then you multiply it to the Jacobian transposed

23:15.520 --> 23:25.000
but basically maps the residual vector into the space of updates of your parameter in

23:25.000 --> 23:26.580
your parameter space.

23:26.580 --> 23:30.840
So it's a form of steepest descent that you're actually performing.

23:30.840 --> 23:36.660
So you can see that solving H plus lambda I delta X equals minus B is a combination

23:36.660 --> 23:39.960
of Gauss-Newton and a steepest descent approach.

23:40.000 --> 23:47.040
So you're basically combining both, and depending on the scale parameter here, you're saying

23:47.040 --> 23:52.640
how strong the steepest descent part of that actually is.

23:52.640 --> 23:56.740
And with this, this is what is called the Levenberg-Marquardt approach, you are actually

23:56.740 --> 24:01.280
able to deal with such systems which are underdetermined.

24:01.280 --> 24:02.520
So how does it work in practice?

24:02.520 --> 24:07.440
So you have an initial guess of your solution X, and then while you're not converged, you

24:07.440 --> 24:13.120
have an initial damping factor you start with, and then you build your linear system,

24:13.120 --> 24:17.640
compute H and B, compute your error vector, you store your error vector to monitor if

24:17.640 --> 24:24.680
your error increases or decreases, and how you change your damping parameter, and then

24:24.680 --> 24:31.840
you solve the sparse system H plus lambda I delta X equals minus B for some sparse solver

24:31.840 --> 24:38.640
that you're using, and then you get your delta X, you add your delta X to the solution,

24:38.640 --> 24:46.600
and then you check, is E smaller than the error of this new solution, and E is the error

24:46.600 --> 24:48.540
that we had before.

24:48.540 --> 24:57.040
If this is the case, then I say, okay, I still have improved my solution, so my, no, sorry,

24:57.040 --> 25:01.200
in this case, the error is increased because this was the previous error and this is a

25:01.200 --> 25:03.200
new error, so the new error is larger.

25:03.200 --> 25:07.020
If this is the case, I'm recovering my old poles and I'm actually increasing my damping

25:07.020 --> 25:11.400
factor, make the damping factor twice as large, and if that's not the case, if I decreased

25:11.400 --> 25:16.320
my error, then I'm actually reducing my damping factor, and then I restart this process.

25:16.320 --> 25:22.360
So what happens is whenever I can improve my solution, I'm actually reducing the damping

25:22.360 --> 25:23.360
factor.

25:23.360 --> 25:27.820
Whenever I increase my error, then I'm recovering the old poles and increasing my damping factor.

25:27.820 --> 25:34.220
So in order to come as close as possible to a solution that approximates H delta X equals

25:34.220 --> 25:35.220
minus B.

25:35.220 --> 25:40.700
And this is also an approach that you typically find when using a bundle adjustment solution.

25:40.700 --> 25:47.540
So bundle adjustment is basically SLAM based on images, so 3D reconstruction based on images,

25:47.540 --> 25:51.660
where you just have a collection of images and feature point that you observe from the

25:51.660 --> 25:52.660
environment.

25:52.660 --> 25:56.300
So bundle adjustment, for example, doesn't assume that you have odometry information,

25:56.300 --> 26:01.740
you just have an unordered set of images where you extract feature points, you assume to

26:01.740 --> 26:05.120
have correspondences between those feature points, and then you want to estimate the

26:05.120 --> 26:09.100
camera locations and the feature points in the environment in 3D.

26:09.100 --> 26:14.900
And what this approach typically does, it minimizes the reprojection error of the feature

26:14.900 --> 26:16.660
points into the 2D image plane.

26:16.660 --> 26:20.600
That means you estimate where your cameras are, you estimate where your 3D points are,

26:20.600 --> 26:25.900
you project the 3D points into the 2D image plane, and then look to the discrepancy between

26:25.900 --> 26:31.900
where the feature is measured or the point is measured and where the feature should be

26:31.900 --> 26:35.480
given this reprojection.

26:35.480 --> 26:40.260
And especially if you have no notation of odometry, you more frequently suffer from

26:40.260 --> 26:43.900
this underdetermined systems and need to address this in an appropriate way.

26:43.900 --> 26:47.460
You need to have enough observations of the same environment in order to have actually

26:47.460 --> 26:52.060
connected graphs, otherwise your graph actually breaks up into several places.

26:52.060 --> 26:57.060
And solutions to this problem exist since quite a long time, so let's say approximately

26:57.060 --> 27:02.660
the 1950s, and photogrammetry approaches have been developed for that.

27:02.660 --> 27:10.100
And you can see this as a special case of the graph-based SLAM problem with landmarks.

27:10.100 --> 27:15.040
In the graph-based SLAM, we typically incorporate more information to this, odometry information,

27:15.040 --> 27:19.420
some other additional information that you can include into this, but structurally they're

27:19.420 --> 27:22.040
actually very, very similar problems.

27:22.040 --> 27:26.320
So to sum that up, what we have discussed here is basically an extension from the postgraph-based

27:26.320 --> 27:31.160
SLAM approach towards general graph-based SLAM approach with landmarks.

27:31.160 --> 27:35.480
So this basically means we're taking our postgraph and extending it with two types of edges and

27:35.480 --> 27:36.880
two types of nodes.

27:36.880 --> 27:42.720
So one node for robot poses, one node for landmark locations, and then we also get two

27:42.720 --> 27:52.040
edges in, and these are the edges that matter for us in the optimization problem.

27:52.040 --> 27:56.640
Then we have talked about the rank of this H-matrix that matters and depends on the Jacobians,

27:56.640 --> 28:03.080
the dimensionality of the Jacobians, and in this landmark-based systems, you more frequently

28:03.080 --> 28:08.520
suffer from this underdetermined system so that typically not Gauss-Newton is used here,

28:08.520 --> 28:13.520
Levenberg-Markov, which is a combination of Gauss-Newton and steepest descent.

28:13.520 --> 28:18.520
And with this, I hope I've given you an idea on how you can actually exploit these graph-based

28:18.520 --> 28:24.320
ideas from postgraph that we have learned to general graphs which also contain landmarks.

28:24.320 --> 28:26.360
Thank you very much for your attention.

